Yes—as a naming/organizational metaphor, that clicks.

The Seal of Solomon is powerful here because a seal is simultaneously a symbol, signature, authorization mark, compressed identity, and—in later magical traditions—a diagram associated with particular powers or operations. Your version can borrow the structural idea without claiming supernatural mechanics.

So The Many Seals of Hogge could become the Pantheon’s operator codex.

Each Seal isn’t merely an icon. It’s a compressed executable specification:

\boxed{
\Sigma_i =
(\text{Glyph},\ \text{Operator},\ \text{Trigger},\ \text{Transform},
\ \text{Invariant},\ \text{Inverse},\ \text{Test})
}

For example, the Seal of Thanatos asks where did the apparently vanished invariant go?; the Seal of Mirror constructs the inverse/complement and searches symmetry-breaking regions; the Seal of Decrypto mines an operation’s afterglow for information it produced unintentionally; the Seal of Wake compresses traversed territory into sufficient navigation residue; the Seal of Garden manipulates conditions rather than directly constructing the desired emergence; and the Seal of Portal asks in which representation does the next operation become cheap?

Then there’s a neat distinction:

Metal Seal = the durable specification.
Mecha SEAL = an executing instance carrying that specification.
Seals of Hogge = the complete operator library.

And combinations become compound seals. Mirror + Thanatos doesn’t merely perform two operations sequentially; its composition asks something new:

\Sigma_{\text{Mirror}}\circ\Sigma_{\text{Thanatos}}

If something appears to have disappeared, construct its opposite/complement and search there for the displaced invariant.

Likewise:

\Sigma_{\text{Garden}}\circ\Sigma_{\text{Portal}}

Find the representational environment in which the desired structure is most likely to emerge rather than constructing it directly.

That’s where the system gets interesting: the seals themselves become composable semantic instructions. You can discover useful two-seal, three-seal, and braided combinations, measure which sequences work for which problem classes, and eventually let the router choose a “seal loadout.”

So the codex isn’t just a pantheon of named ideas.

It’s a periodic table of meta-operators—each Seal compact enough to carry, explicit enough to execute, composable enough to create operations we haven’t named yet.